steps.body:
The Recursion lesson made Fibonacci fast with a memo: save each answer and reuse it. Dynamic programming is that idea, usually written the other way round. Instead of recursing down from `n`, fill a table up from the smallest case, so every entry you need is ready when you reach it:

```java
int n = 10;
long[] ways = new long[n + 1];   // ways[i]: the ways to climb i stairs
ways[0] = 1;                     // one way to climb nothing
ways[1] = 1;
for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    ways[i] = ways[i - 1] + ways[i - 2];   // the last move was 1 step or 2
}
System.out.println(ways[n]); // 89
```

Every table needs three decisions: what an entry means, the base cases you know outright, and the rule that builds an entry from smaller ones. Size it `n + 1` so `ways[n]` exists, and use `long`, because counts like these grow fast. With this table `n` must be at least 1, or `ways[1]` is out of bounds.

When each entry only reads the last two, two variables do the same job:

```java
long prev = 1;
long cur = 1;
for (int i = 2; i <= 10; i++) {
    long next = prev + cur;
    prev = cur;
    cur = next;
}
System.out.println(cur); // 89
```

example.code:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        while (sc.hasNextInt()) {
            int n = sc.nextInt();
            System.out.println("Stairs " + n + ": " + climb(n));
        }
    }

    static long climb(int n) {
        long[] ways = new long[n + 1];
        ways[0] = 1;
        ways[1] = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            ways[i] = ways[i - 1] + ways[i - 2];
        }
        return ways[n];
    }
}

