import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int k = sc.nextInt();
        boolean[] broken = new boolean[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            broken[sc.nextInt()] = true;
        }
        long[] ways = new long[n + 1];
        ways[0] = 1;
        ways[1] = broken[1] ? 0 : 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            ways[i] = broken[i] ? 0 : ways[i - 1] + ways[i - 2];
        }
        StringBuilder table = new StringBuilder("Table:");
        for (long w : ways) {
            table.append(' ').append(w);
        }
        System.out.println(table);
        System.out.println("Ways: " + ways[n]);
    }
}

steps.body:
Many rules are a choice between two options. A thief walks down a street and can't rob two houses next to each other. Let `best[i]` be the most money from the first `i` houses. For house `i`, either skip it and keep `best[i - 1]`, or take it, which rules out its neighbor, for `best[i - 2]` plus its money. The entry is the larger of the two:

```java
int[] money = {2, 7, 9, 3, 1};
int[] best = new int[money.length + 1];   // best[0] is 0: no houses, no money
best[1] = money[0];
for (int i = 2; i <= money.length; i++) {
    best[i] = Math.max(best[i - 1], best[i - 2] + money[i - 1]);   // skip, or take
}
System.out.println(best[money.length]); // 12, from 2 + 9 + 1
```

The table is one longer than the array, so `best[i]` covers houses 0 to `i - 1`, and house `i` in the rule is `money[i - 1]`. That shift is the usual source of off-by-one bugs, so write down what an entry means before you write the loop. Guard the empty street too: with no houses, `best[1]` doesn't exist.

To run the same rule on part of the array, give the method a range, `from` and `to`, and read `money[from + i - 1]` instead.

