import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int[] money = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            money[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }
        System.out.println("Street: " + rob(money, 0, n - 1));
        int circle = n == 1 ? money[0] : Math.max(rob(money, 0, n - 2), rob(money, 1, n - 1));
        System.out.println("Circle: " + circle);
    }

    static int rob(int[] money, int from, int to) {
        int n = to - from + 1;
        int[] best = new int[n + 1];
        best[1] = money[from];
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            best[i] = Math.max(best[i - 1], best[i - 2] + money[from + i - 1]);
        }
        return best[n];
    }
}

steps.body:
For the fewest coins that make an amount, let `dp[a]` be the fewest coins for amount `a`. The last coin was one of the coins, so try each one, `dp[a - coin] + 1`, and keep the smallest. Fill from 1 up, since every entry reads smaller amounts:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

int[] coins = {1, 2, 5};
int amount = 11;
int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);             // more coins than any real answer
dp[0] = 0;
for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
    for (int coin : coins) {
        if (coin <= a) {
            dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], dp[a - coin] + 1);
        }
    }
}
System.out.println(dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount]); // 3, from 5 + 5 + 1
```

`amount + 1` stands for impossible, since no answer needs more coins than the amount. Don't use `Integer.MAX_VALUE` for it: `dp[a - coin] + 1` would overflow to a negative number and win every `Math.min`.

Taking the biggest coin first, the greedy way, can fail. With coins 1, 3, and 4 and an amount of 6, it takes 4 + 1 + 1, while 3 + 3 needs only two. The table tries every last coin, so it can't miss the better choice.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int k = sc.nextInt();
        int[] coins = new int[k];
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            coins[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }
        int q = sc.nextInt();
        for (int t = 0; t < q; t++) {
            int amount = sc.nextInt();
            System.out.println(amount + ": " + fewest(coins, amount));
        }
    }

    static int fewest(int[] coins, int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
            for (int coin : coins) {
                if (coin <= a) {
                    dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], dp[a - coin] + 1);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount];
    }
}

