steps.body:
Some problems need two numbers to describe a smaller version, like a row and a column, so the table is 2D. To count the paths through a grid that move only right or down, let `paths[r][c]` be the ways to reach cell `(r, c)`. You arrive from above or from the left, so add those two. A wall has 0 paths:

```java
char[][] grid = {"...".toCharArray(), ".#.".toCharArray(), "...".toCharArray()};
int rows = grid.length;
int cols = grid[0].length;
long[][] paths = new long[rows][cols];
for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
    for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
        if (grid[r][c] == '#') {
            paths[r][c] = 0;
        } else if (r == 0 && c == 0) {
            paths[r][c] = 1;
        } else {
            long up = r > 0 ? paths[r - 1][c] : 0;
            long left = c > 0 ? paths[r][c - 1] : 0;
            paths[r][c] = up + left;
        }
    }
}
System.out.println(paths[rows - 1][cols - 1]); // 2
```

Filling row by row, left to right, means the cells above and to the left are always done before you need them. The `r > 0` and `c > 0` checks stand in for cells off the grid, which have no paths. The same table answers questions other than counting: change `up + left` to `Math.min(up, left)` plus the cell's cost and it finds the cheapest path instead. Off the grid then has to mean "no way in", not 0, so take only the side that exists.

example.code:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int rows = sc.nextInt();
        int cols = sc.nextInt();
        char[][] grid = new char[rows][];
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            grid[r] = sc.next().toCharArray();
        }

        long[][] paths = new long[rows][cols];
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] == '#') {
                    paths[r][c] = 0;
                } else if (r == 0 && c == 0) {
                    paths[r][c] = 1;
                } else {
                    long up = r > 0 ? paths[r - 1][c] : 0;
                    long left = c > 0 ? paths[r][c - 1] : 0;
                    paths[r][c] = up + left;
                }
            }
        }
        for (long[] row : paths) {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (long p : row) {
                sb.append(String.format("%3d", p));
            }
            System.out.println(sb);
        }
        System.out.println("Paths: " + paths[rows - 1][cols - 1]);
    }
}

